      "writtenOn": "2024-01-25T21:04:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "IMO, this would be much easier to read by making use of `char`:\n```\nfn is_ascii_alphanumeric_or_in(c: char, chars: \u0026str) -\u003e bool {\n    c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || chars.contains(c)\n}\n\nfn is_valid_dt_node_name(name: \u0026[u8]) -\u003e bool {\n    name.iter().all(|c| is_ascii_alphanumeric_or_in((*c).into(), \",._+-@\"))\n}\n\nfn is_valid_dt_prop_name(name: \u0026[u8]) -\u003e bool {\n    name.iter().all(|c| is_ascii_alphanumeric_or_in((*c).into(), \",._+-#?\"))\n}\n```\n\nHowever, I\u0027m not convinced of the need for this: the DT is trusted (because we trust our kernel and our kernel trusts its bootloader) so what is this extra complexity helping with? Prevent UB in the libfdt calls?\n\nIf you do believe that this validation is helpful, then it should be implemented properly as you\u0027re missing some rules (quoting from the DTSpec):\n\n\u003e The `node-name` component [...] shall be 1 to 31 characters in length [...]\n\n\u003e The `node-name` shall start with a lower or uppercase character [...]\n\nand the `@`, if present, must be unique and can\u0027t be at the end\n\n\u003e Each node [...] is named [...] `node-name@unit-address`\n\nFurthermore (more complicated to check than just parsing the string)\n\n\u003e The `unit-address` must match the first address specified in the `reg` property\nof the node. If the node has no `reg` property, the `@unit-address` must be omitted\n\nand (even harder to validate)\n\n\u003e The binding for a particular bus may specify additional, more specific\nrequirements for the format of [...] the `unit-address`.\n\n\u003e The `node-name` [...] should describe the general class of device.\n\n(last one\u0027s a joke)\n\nSame for properties:\n\n\u003e Property names are strings of 1 to 31 characters [...]\n\n\u003e Nonstandard property names should specify a unique string prefix [...]\n\nAnd what about validating the _values_ of the properties?\n\nSo instead of having some half-baked validation, I\u0027d recommend we trust the kernel and just take the tree format for granted i.e. remove these checks; WDYT?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 24,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 45,
        "endChar": 1
