      "writtenOn": "2024-01-25T21:04:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Doesn\u0027t this stack have the invariant that it will always contain entries of the form\n```\n(Path(\"/path/to/device-tree/a/b/c\"), CString(\"/a/b/c\"))\n```\n?\n\nIf I\u0027m not mistaken, it would be simpler for your code to only deal with `Path`s and only create `CString`s when calling into `libfdt`. The `stack` would only contain simple `Path` entries instead of pairs i.e.\n```\nwhile let Some(path) \u003d stack.pop() {\n```\nand you\u0027d get the DT path from the FS path:\n```\n    let fdt_path \u003d path.strip_prefix(\u0026fs_root)...?;\n    let fdt_path \u003d CString::new(fdt_path.as_os_str().as_bytes())...?;\n    let mut node \u003d fdt.node_mut(\u0026fdt_path)...?;\n```\nwhile the entries are (obv) at the FS path:\n```\n    let entries \u003d fs::read_dir(\u0026path)...?;\n```\nthen, you can use the path as-is when parsing the entries:\n```\n        if entry_type.is_dir() {\n            // Validate entry.name() here, if you believe that\u0027s necessary.\n            stack.push(entry.path());\n        } else if entry_type.is_file() {\n            let name \u003d CString::new(entry.name().as_bytes())...?;\n            // Validate name here, if you believe that\u0027s necessary.\n            let value \u003d fs::read(\u0026entry.path())?;\n            node.setprop(\u0026name, \u0026value)...?;\n        } else { ... }\n```\n\n(same comment for your test)",
